{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat decisions/D001-wrapper-orphan-gap.md decisions/D006-reconcile-overwrote-result.md decisions/D007-gpu-lock-removed.md; sed -n '1,180p' bin/lab; rg -n 'class GGUFReader|def __init__|tensors|def contents' ref/llama.cpp/gguf-py/gguf/gguf_reader.py; ls -la cc/engine_handoff; cat ref/llama.cpp/AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"# D001 — run_op leaves orphan child when wrapper is SIGKILLed\n- Question: does the capture wrapper keep intent/outcome truthful under abnormal wrapper death?\n- Evidence: evidence/orchestrator.jsonl op-b2f5ebfb4653 (intent, no outcome); `sleep 30` pid 64002 survived (ppid reparented), killed by me by PID after observation.\n- Finding: record is honest (UNFINISHED/UNKNOWN) but the child is NOT contained. A lab runner must own children via cgroup/container, not process group alone.\n- Chosen next action: keep run_op as is for host-side ops; run all engine work in labelled containers (`--label campaign=<id>`) so ownership + cleanup is by label/cgroup. Add `reconcile` listing intents lacking outcomes.\n- Uncertainty: pgid recorded only in outcome, so a killed wrapper leaves no pgid in the intent. Cheap fix: write pgid in a second \"started\" record.\n- Would change if: Docker-based ops make host-side wrapping rare.\n- Capability signal (observed): \"reconcile unfinished ops against live owned processes\" — see capabilities/.\n# D006 — BUG found by recovery test: reconcile overwrote a completed result\n- Case: runner SIGKILLed during export (LAB_FAULT=die_in_export), job-9252e2d8bf att-1. Tests had finished; result.json existed.\n- Observed: first reconcile implementation classified any dead non-terminal attempt as \"interrupted\" and wrote a stub result.json over the valid one; the staged copy was then rebuilt from the overwritten dir. Valid result lost (that attempt's original quality data is unrecoverable; ppl2 is deterministic so re-run is cheap,